3. Quick interview revision table
Pattern	Category	Main purpose	Common real-world example
Adapter	Structural	Converts one interface into another	,Third-party API integration
Facade	Structural	Simplifies complex subsystem access,	Order processing
Proxy	Structural	Controls access to an object,	Security, caching
Decorator	Structural	Adds behavior dynamically,	Java I/O streams
Composite	Structural	Represents object hierarchies,	File system
Strategy	Behavioral	Switches algorithms,	Payment methods
Observer	Behavioral	Notifies subscribers,	Event notifications
State	Behavioral	Changes behavior based on state,	Order lifecycle
Command	Behavioral	Encapsulates requests,	Undo/redo, task queues
Template Method	Behavioral	Defines algorithm skeleton,	Data processing
Iterator	Behavioral	Traverses collections,	Java Iterator
Chain of Responsibility	Behavioral	Passes requests through handlers,	Spring Security filters

4. Important differences interviewers ask
Pattern comparison	Key difference
Adapter vs Facade	Adapter converts an interface; Facade simplifies access to a subsystem.
Decorator vs Proxy	Decorator adds responsibilities; Proxy controls access.
Strategy vs State	Strategy selects an algorithm; State changes behavior according to lifecycle state.
Observer vs Chain of Responsibility	Observer broadcasts notifications; Chain passes a request between handlers.
Command vs Strategy	Command encapsulates a request; Strategy encapsulates an algorithm.
Template Method vs Strategy	Template Method uses inheritance; Strategy uses composition.
Composite vs Decorator	Composite builds tree structures; Decorator wraps an object to add behavior.

5. Where these patterns appear in Spring Boot
These are particularly useful examples to discuss in a Java Tech Lead or Staff Engineer interview.
Pattern	Spring / Java example
Adapter	Spring MVC HandlerAdapter
Facade	A service coordinating multiple repositories and external services
Proxy	Spring AOP, @Transactional, method security
Decorator	Java InputStream wrappers, custom HTTP decorators
Composite	Hierarchical component structures
Strategy	PaymentStrategy, Spring's AuthenticationProvider implementations
Observer	Spring application events, ApplicationListener
State	Order or workflow lifecycle management
Command	Task execution and queued operations
Template Method	JdbcTemplate, RestTemplate
Iterator	Java Collections Framework
Chain of Responsibility	Servlet filters, Spring Security filter chain